## The question

Cycle 885's checker showed the declared window requirements (content-
only, equivariance, permanence monotonicity, non-constancy) admit
every lattice dilation of the record support, and the residual was
priced as "a dilation scale plus a centre convention". This block asks
the two questions that pricing begs: how big is the surviving map
space REALLY (Q1), and does any byte-quotable axiom clause select the
support among its members (Q2)? Same 12-configuration family and group
as Cycle 885 (family digests match; all seven of 885's headline
numbers reproduce before anything new runs), exact arithmetic
throughout.

## Q1 — the freedom is not a scale (closure claim REFUTED)

- **Sufficiency theorem.** For every finite nonempty rotation-invariant
  structuring set S, the Minkowski inflation W_S(R) = supp(R) (+) S
  satisfies every declared requirement — proved structurally
  (translation-equivariance and monotonicity are automatic for
  Minkowski sums; rotation-equivariance is exactly rotation-invariance
  of S) and verified exhaustively (all 15 such S in the radius-1 box
  through the full 1,440-check harness; all 1,023 in radius 2
  certified).
- **Injectivity lemma.** The family contains a lone record at the
  origin and W_S(single) = S, so S determines the map: the count of
  distinct admissible Minkowski windows IS the count of rotation-
  invariant sets — **15 / 1,023 / 2,097,151** at box radius 1 / 2 / 3,
  unbounded overall. Cycle 885's dilations are 3 of the 1,023.
- **Necessity FAILS — the space is bigger still.** Admissible
  constructions provably outside every fixed-S form: erosions (windows
  strictly INSIDE the support, empty on most configurations), bounding
  boxes and axis closures, inflations keyed on record size or on the
  RECORD CONTENT itself, unions of admissible maps — and the checker
  added rank/threshold filters (an infinite family in which dilations
  are only the t = 1 members), morphological closing, and bitcount-
  and diameter-keyed variants. The surviving space is at least the
  union of four named classes, closed under union and composition with
  inflations, and this block claims NO closure of its own.
- **The annular chart is strictly coarser, with an exact index**:
  complete enumeration of all 1,023 radius-2 Minkowski maps collapses
  1,023 distinct set-valued behaviours onto 113 annular (a, b)
  readings on the primary family (220 on the checker's fresh family —
  the ratio is family-dependent, the strict coarseness is not).

## Q2 — selection: one derivation, one absence, one gauge

Every selector is computed as a filter WITH its grounding byte-quoted
from the pinned texts, and quote-to-computation fidelity was the
checker's top refutation target (needle-tampering is a tooth):

- **Count-once / no-phantom-registration: NOT GROUNDED.** The quoted
  sentence is a multiplicity bound on sites that DO carry a record; it
  says nothing about zero-record sites, and "window" / "detector" do
  not occur in it. Reading it as "the window may contain only record
  sites" is an addition, not a quotation. k = 0 is NOT derived.
- **Readout additivity: DERIVES support containment (the lower
  bound).** The clause "only records are readable… additive over
  pairwise-disjoint records" is quoted verbatim and turned into a
  dichotomy: decomposing into singletons, translation equivariance
  forces EITHER the window to contain its records on every
  configuration OR to miss them entirely — and the second branch is
  refuted computationally (the origin-free inflation violates
  additivity on 16/23 decompositions). So supp(R) is contained in W(R)
  is an axiom consequence. But every containing window gives the SAME
  readout, so the clause is silent on the upper bound — it cannot
  pick k.
- **Minimality: ABSENT.** Across all three pinned texts, zero hits of
  minimality-as-selection language (the raw hits are titles and
  metadata; both Gate-B notes contain none at all). Minimality would
  be a NEW supplied convention, not an axiom consequence — reported as
  the finding it is.
- **The Gate-B texts themselves are grounded AND negative**: they
  declare the window supplied and open.

**The correction to Cycle 885's inference, stated exactly.** 885 read
the boundary shell's permanence failure as "permanence SELECTS the
support". That inference does not generalize: the boundary shell
fails because it is a DIFFERENCE (inflation minus support); the pure
inflation by the same offsets is monotone, admissible — and wholly
disjoint from the support on some configurations. Permanence forbids
RETRACTION, not DISPLACEMENT. Support containment does hold, but it
comes from the additivity clause, not from permanence. (Containment
fails as a REQ-consequence by 511 explicit structuring-set witnesses,
checker-generalized.)

## The gauge theorem (the block's sharpest positive)

Nine of the twelve admissible catalogue maps give an IDENTICAL
axiom-level scalar readout on every configuration: once the window
contains the records, the only observable the axioms supply cannot see
the extent at all. The window extent is a GAUGE direction of the
axiom-level readout; it becomes physical only through the Gate-B
semantics the pinned note itself calls supplied — and the annular
(a, b) chart moves with the gauge, so (a, b) carries information the
axioms cannot certify.

